From 00fb42becbfbdd0aee4e6af9940e72655cec8108 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 15:54:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 083/105] demux: write down what a seek leaves behind and what
 the return values mean

Both were undefined, and where a contract is undefined each demuxer guesses:
this series has fixed the same seek-stale timestamp in eight modules, the
same sticky end of file in a dozen, a pcr reset on a seek path that undid the
input's own non-flushing repeat, and a title change that was a bare stream
seek next to a sibling case doing the full cleanup. Each of those was a
demuxer differing from its neighbours rather than a demuxer being wrong on
its own terms, so the fix that stops the next one is to say what the rule is.

Nothing here is new behaviour. It is what the modules that were already right
do, and what the ones that were fixed now do.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 include/vlc_demux.h | 56 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 54 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/include/vlc_demux.h b/include/vlc_demux.h
index f359f547fa..97c6c0063b 100644
--- a/include/vlc_demux.h
+++ b/include/vlc_demux.h
@@ -97,7 +97,26 @@ struct demux_t
     input_thread_t *p_input;
 };
 
-/* pf_demux return values */
+/**
+ * pf_demux return values.
+ *
+ * VLC_DEMUXER_SUCCESS says the demuxer made progress: it consumed input, and
+ * usually handed something to the output. It does not have to hand anything
+ * over on any given call, but a demuxer that can neither progress nor say why
+ * must not sit in this state - the input calls it again immediately, so a
+ * demuxer with nothing to do has to report one of the two below or wait.
+ *
+ * VLC_DEMUXER_EOF says there is no more data. It is not an error and it is not
+ * final: the input may seek and demux again, so a demuxer must leave itself in
+ * a state a seek can recover from, and must clear whatever it latched to
+ * remember the end. It is the only return that lets a repeat or a next item
+ * happen, which is why a transient starve must not use it.
+ *
+ * VLC_DEMUXER_EGENERIC says the stream cannot be read any further. The input
+ * puts the whole playback into an error state, so it is for damage, not for
+ * running off the end of the file and not for a packet that failed to parse
+ * when the next one might.
+ */
 #define VLC_DEMUXER_EOF       0
 #define VLC_DEMUXER_EGENERIC -1
 #define VLC_DEMUXER_SUCCESS   1
@@ -233,7 +252,40 @@ enum demux_query_e
     /* I. Common queries to access_demux and demux */
     /* POSITION double between 0.0 and 1.0 */
     DEMUX_GET_POSITION = 0x300, /* arg1= double *       res=    */
-    DEMUX_SET_POSITION,         /* arg1= double arg2= bool b_precise    res=can fail    */
+
+    /** Seek to a fraction of the stream.
+     *
+     * What a seek has to leave behind, which holds for DEMUX_SET_TIME and for
+     * DEMUX_SET_TITLE / DEMUX_SET_SEEKPOINT just as much - those reposition
+     * the stream and are seeks whoever asked for them calls them:
+     *
+     * - no timestamp derived from where the stream used to be. Anything the
+     *   demuxer accumulated to date its output, and anything it reports
+     *   through DEMUX_GET_TIME, is re-established from the new position or
+     *   invalidated. A demuxer that carries its own timeline across a seek
+     *   rather than re-deriving it from the new position must carry it whole.
+     * - the elementary streams told their data is not continuous, by
+     *   BLOCK_FLAG_DISCONTINUITY on the first block sent for each of them.
+     *   The flag describes the seam and nothing else: whoever acts on it
+     *   clears it, so that the rest of the block it arrived on is not treated
+     *   as a second break.
+     * - nothing queued from before the seek still waiting to be sent.
+     *
+     * A demuxer serving a seek must not reset the output's pcr itself. The
+     * input has already done whatever reset the seek needs by the time this
+     * is called, and on a seek that deliberately does not flush - a repeat in
+     * place - ES_OUT_RESET_PCR undoes it and empties the output. It stays the
+     * right thing to say where the timeline really does restart underneath a
+     * playing stream, such as a period boundary or a detected discontinuity.
+     *
+     * b_precise asks for the requested position exactly. When it is false the
+     * demuxer may land on the nearest point it can reach cheaply. When it is
+     * true and the format cannot start there - a keyframe, a frame boundary -
+     * the demuxer seeks before the requested position and tells the output
+     * what to discard with ES_OUT_SET_NEXT_DISPLAY_TIME.
+     *
+     * arg1= double arg2= bool b_precise    res=can fail */
+    DEMUX_SET_POSITION,
 
     /* LENGTH/TIME in microsecond, 0 if unknown */
     DEMUX_GET_LENGTH,           /* arg1= int64_t *      res=    */
